Subject: [overlay 0030] V_SuppressFrameRender — a constant render count per host frame

The visionOS immersive shell renders the frame once per eye. CL_Frame draws too, whenever
cl_maxfps did not throttle the tick, so per host frame the engine ran R_RenderFrame two or
three times and eye 0 was sometimes the FIRST render and sometimes the SECOND, alternating
at the throttle beat, while eye 1 was always the last. R_RenderFrame is not idempotent for
dynamic lighting (glr.dlightframe advances per render, surf->dlightframe is consumed, and
GL_PushLights re-uploads only what that render's node walk drew), so the two eyes saw
different lighting states: flicker, worse in one eye, and a wall lit on one half.

V_SuppressFrameRender(true) makes CL_Frame's SCR_UpdateScreen call site skip the draw and
nothing else — sound, input, prediction, cinematic decode and the ref_frame accounting all
still run. The shell wraps Qcommon_Frame in it and then renders exactly eye 0 then eye 1,
so eye 0 is always render #1 and eye 1 always render #2. Non-VR paths never set it.

Apply: patch -p1 -d vendor/q2repro < this
--- a/src/client/view.c
+++ b/src/client/view.c
@@ -40,6 +40,25 @@
     return r;
 }
 
+// [vr overlay 0030] Suppress CL_Frame's own SCR_UpdateScreen for the duration of one
+// Qcommon_Frame. The visionOS immersive shell needs the render COUNT per host frame to be
+// constant: CL_Frame draws whenever cl_maxfps did not throttle, so the shell's eye 0 was
+// sometimes the first R_RenderFrame of the host frame and sometimes the second, while eye 1
+// was always the last. R_RenderFrame is not idempotent for dynamic lighting, so an eye that
+// alternates position in that sequence sees a different lighting state from its partner —
+// the flicker, worse in one eye. With this set, Qcommon_Frame still runs everything else it
+// runs (sound, input, prediction, cinematic decode, the ref_frame accounting); only the draw
+// is skipped, and the shell then renders exactly eye 0 then eye 1 itself.
+static bool v_suppress_render;
+void V_SuppressFrameRender(bool suppress)
+{
+    v_suppress_render = suppress;
+}
+bool V_RenderSuppressed(void)
+{
+    return v_suppress_render;
+}
+
 //=============
 
 static cvar_t   *cl_add_particles;
--- a/src/client/client.h
+++ b/src/client/client.h
@@ -916,6 +916,10 @@
 void V_Init(void);
 void V_Shutdown(void);
 void V_RenderView(void);
+// [vr overlay 0030] see view.c — the immersive shell suppresses CL_Frame's own draw so the
+// per-host-frame render count is constant.
+void V_SuppressFrameRender(bool suppress);
+bool V_RenderSuppressed(void);
 void V_AddEntity(const entity_t *ent);
 void V_AddParticle(const particle_t *p);
 void V_AddLightEx(cl_shadow_light_t *light);
--- a/src/client/main.c
+++ b/src/client/main.c
@@ -3423,7 +3423,11 @@
         if (host_speeds->integer)
             time_before_ref = Sys_Milliseconds();
 
-        SCR_UpdateScreen();
+        // [vr overlay 0030] The immersive shell renders both eyes itself, immediately after
+        // this frame returns; drawing here as well would make the host frame's render count
+        // vary with the cl_maxfps throttle. Everything else in the frame still happens.
+        if (!V_RenderSuppressed())
+            SCR_UpdateScreen();
 
         if (host_speeds->integer)
             time_after_ref = Sys_Milliseconds();
